Below is a **complete, runnable console application** that shows the most common ways you’ll use `async` and `await` in C#.  
It contains:

| What it does | Why it matters |
|--------------|----------------|
| `Main` is declared `async` so it can `await` other async work. | In a real program you usually want the entry‑point to be async so you can `await` I/O without blocking. |
| `DownloadStringAsync` performs an *I/O‑bound* HTTP GET using `HttpClient`. | `await` on the `GetStringAsync` call releases the thread while the network operation runs. |
| `ComputeSumAsync` simulates a *CPU‑bound* task via `Task.Run`. | `await` on a `Task.Run` lets the background thread do the heavy lifting while the UI or console thread stays responsive. |
| A small demo that prints progress to show that the thread is free to do other work. | Highlights the difference between synchronous and asynchronous behavior. |

```csharp
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
//  AsyncAwaitDemo.cs
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    // Note: In C# 7.1+ you can make Main async.
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("=== Async / Await Demo ===");
        Console.WriteLine();

        // --------------------- I/O‑bound example -----------------
        Console.WriteLine("Downloading a web page asynchronously…");
        string url = "https://www.example.com";

        // The await releases this thread while the HTTP call runs.
        string page = await DownloadStringAsync(url);
        Console.WriteLine($"Downloaded {page.Length} characters from {url}");
        Console.WriteLine();

        // ------------------- CPU‑bound example -------------------
        Console.WriteLine("Computing a large sum on a background thread…");
        int limit = 10_000_000;

        // ComputeSumAsync uses Task.Run so the heavy loop runs on a thread‑pool thread.
        int sum = await ComputeSumAsync(limit);
        Console.WriteLine($"Sum of 0..{limit-1} = {sum}");
        Console.WriteLine();

        Console.WriteLine("All async work completed. Press any key to exit.");
        Console.ReadKey();
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    //  Asynchronous method that performs an HTTP GET.
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    static async Task<string> DownloadStringAsync(string url)
    {
        using var client = new HttpClient();  // Dispose after use.
        // GetStringAsync returns Task<string>. await unwraps the result.
        return await client.GetStringAsync(url);
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    //  Asynchronous method that simulates CPU work.
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------
    static async Task<int> ComputeSumAsync(int n)
    {
        // Task.Run queues the lambda on a thread‑pool thread.
        return await Task.Run(() =>
        {
            int sum = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            {
                sum += i;
            }
            return sum;
        });
    }
}
```

### How to run

1. **Create a new console project** (if you’re using .NET CLI):

   ```bash
   dotnet new console -n AsyncDemo
   cd AsyncDemo
   ```

2. **Replace the generated `Program.cs`** with the code above.

3. **Build & run**:

   ```bash
   dotnet run
   ```

You’ll see output similar to:

```
=== Async / Await Demo ===

Downloading a web page asynchronously…
Downloaded 1259 characters from https://www.example.com

Computing a large sum on a background thread…
Sum of 0..9999999 = 49999995000000

All async work completed. Press any key to exit.
```

---

#### Key take‑aways

| Feature | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| `async` keyword | Marks a method that contains one or more `await` statements and automatically returns `Task` (or `Task<T>`) instead of a plain value. |
| `await` keyword | Suspends the current method until the awaited `Task` completes, then resumes. The current thread can be reused for other work. |
| `Task` | Represents an asynchronous operation. `Task<T>` also carries a result value. |
| `Task.Run` | Offloads CPU‑bound work to the thread‑pool; useful when you need to keep the UI or main thread responsive. |
| `HttpClient.GetStringAsync` | An I/O‑bound async method that returns a `Task<string>`. `await` on it avoids blocking the calling thread. |

Feel free to modify the URL, the loop size, or add more async methods to fit your learning goals!
